## Introduction

Control structures allow a program to make decisions and repeat actions. The if-else statement executes different code based on a condition. The switch statement selects one of many code blocks based on a value. These are the building blocks of logic in C.
## Key Concepts

- if: execute code only if a condition is true
- if-else: execute one block if true, another if false
- if-else if-else: check multiple conditions in order
- switch: select one of many blocks based on a single value (int or char only)
- break in switch: exits the switch — without it, execution falls through to the next case
- default in switch: executed if no case matches
- Ternary operator: condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false (shorthand for if-else)

## Important Formulas

- `if: if (condition) { /* code */ }`
- `if-else: if (condition) { /* A */ } else { /* B */ }`
- `switch: switch (var) { case 1: ...; break; case 2: ...; break; default: ...; }`
- `Ternary: result = (a > b) ? a : b;`

## Comparison Tables

### if-else vs switch

| Feature | if-else | switch |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Condition type | Any expression (>, <, ==, &&, ||) | Equality only (== with int/char) |
| Data types | int, float, char, etc. | int and char only (not float or string) |
| Range checking | Yes (if (x >= 0 && x <= 100)) | No (each case is a single value) |
| Fall-through | No | Yes (if break is missing) |
| Best for | Complex conditions, ranges | Many discrete values (menus, grades) |

> Use switch for menu-driven programs. Use if-else for complex conditions and ranges.

## Code Examples

### Grade Calculator using if-else if

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int marks;
    printf("Enter your marks (0-100): ");
    scanf("%d", &marks);

    if (marks >= 90) {
        printf("Grade: A+\n");
    } else if (marks >= 80) {
        printf("Grade: A\n");
    } else if (marks >= 70) {
        printf("Grade: B\n");
    } else if (marks >= 60) {
        printf("Grade: C\n");
    } else if (marks >= 50) {
        printf("Grade: D\n");
    } else {
        printf("Grade: F (Fail)\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The conditions are checked top to bottom. The first true condition's block is executed, and the rest are skipped. The final 'else' is the fallback when no condition matches.

### Simple Calculator using switch

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    char operator;
    float a, b;

    printf("Enter expression (e.g., 5 + 3): ");
    scanf("%f %c %f", &a, &operator, &b);

    switch (operator) {
        case '+':
            printf("%.2f + %.2f = %.2f\n", a, b, a + b);
            break;
        case '-':
            printf("%.2f - %.2f = %.2f\n", a, b, a - b);
            break;
        case '*':
            printf("%.2f * %.2f = %.2f\n", a, b, a * b);
            break;
        case '/':
            if (b != 0)
                printf("%.2f / %.2f = %.2f\n", a, b, a / b);
            else
                printf("Error: Division by zero!\n");
            break;
        default:
            printf("Invalid operator!\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```

switch checks the operator character. Each case handles one operator. break prevents fall-through. default handles invalid operators. Note the division-by-zero check inside the '/' case.


## Summary

Control structures allow a program to make decisions and repeat actions. The if-else statement executes different code based on a condition. The switch statement selects one of many code blocks based on a value. These are the building blocks of logic in C.

