## Introduction

Loops repeat a block of code multiple times. The 'for' loop is used when you know the number of iterations. The 'while' loop checks the condition BEFORE executing (may run 0 times). The 'do-while' loop checks AFTER executing (runs at least once). Loops are essential for processing arrays, generating patterns, and repeating calculations.
## Key Concepts

- for loop: for (init; condition; update) { } — used when count is known
- while loop: while (condition) { } — checks BEFORE, may not execute at all
- do-while loop: do { } while (condition) — checks AFTER, runs at least once
- break: exits the loop immediately
- continue: skips the rest of the current iteration, goes to the next
- Nested loops: a loop inside another loop — used for 2D patterns, matrix operations
- Infinite loop: condition is always true — use break to exit

## Important Formulas

- `for: for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { /* runs n times */ }`
- `while: while (condition) { /* runs while true */ }`
- `do-while: do { /* runs at least once */ } while (condition);`
- `Nested loop iterations: outer × inner = total iterations`

## Comparison Tables

### Comparison of Loop Types

| Feature | for | while | do-while |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| When to use | Known count | Unknown count, check first | Unknown count, run at least once |
| Condition check | Before each iteration | Before each iteration | After each iteration |
| Min executions | 0 | 0 | 1 |
| Syntax | for(init;cond;update){} | while(cond){} | do{}while(cond); |
| Best example | Array traversal | Reading until sentinel | Menu display |

> All three loops can be converted to each other, but each has its most natural use case.

## Code Examples

### Three Loops — Same Task (Print 1 to 5)

```c
// Using for loop (when count is known)
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);  // 1 2 3 4 5
}

// Using while loop (check before)
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    printf("%d ", i);  // 1 2 3 4 5
    i++;
}

// Using do-while loop (check after, runs at least once)
int j = 1;
do {
    printf("%d ", j);  // 1 2 3 4 5
    j++;
} while (j <= 5);
```

All three produce the same output. The for loop is the most compact. The while loop separates initialization, condition, and update. The do-while guarantees at least one execution even if the condition is false initially.

### Star Pattern using Nested Loops

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int n = 5;

    // Print a right-angle triangle of stars
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {       // outer: rows
        for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {   // inner: columns
            printf("* ");
        }
        printf("\n");  // new line after each row
    }

    return 0;
}

/* Output:
*
* *
* * *
* * * *
* * * * *
*/
```

The outer loop controls rows (1 to 5). The inner loop controls columns (1 to i). In row 1, print 1 star. In row 2, print 2 stars. And so on. The inner loop's limit depends on the outer loop variable — this is the key to triangular patterns.


## Summary

Loops repeat a block of code multiple times. The 'for' loop is used when you know the number of iterations. The 'while' loop checks the condition BEFORE executing (may run 0 times). The 'do-while' loop checks AFTER executing (runs at least once). Loops are essential for processing arrays, generating patterns, and repeating calculations.

